# The two device nodes a LineXinBar session opens directly.
#
# Everything else about input arrives through the compositor, which reads
# `/dev/input` on a seat logind has already granted it. These two are the
# exceptions, and neither is granted to anybody by default — so without this
# file the shell's controller handling half works on the machine it was written
# for, and says nothing about why.
#
# `uaccess` rather than a group: it grants the device to whoever holds the
# active session on the seat, which is the person sitting there and at no point
# every account on the machine. Numbered below 73 so the ACL is applied by
# systemd's own 73-seat-late.rules, which is what acts on the tag.
#
# What that narrows is *which account*, and nothing whatever about which
# program. The ACL names a uid and every process of that uid is inside it: this
# shell, and equally Valve's client, every game it starts, whatever the software
# hub installed. A game cannot bind `lxb_shell_v1` — the compositor checks the
# pid the kernel stamped on the connection — but with `/dev/uinput` it can
# publish a keyboard and type, and nothing downstream can tell those events from
# the real keyboard's, because by then there is nothing to tell apart. This is
# the one place the session's rule that games are untrusted clients does not
# hold, and it is a property of the ACL rather than an oversight in it.
#
# Closing it means a component the games are not — something running as root
# that opens these nodes and hands the descriptor to a process it has
# authenticated — and there is none. Moving the open into the compositor would
# not do it: the compositor runs as the same user, and takes its devices through
# libseat, which asks logind, which hands out only what is assigned to the seat.
# `/dev/uinput` is `misc` with a static node and is on no seat. See the
# Permissions section of the README, which says the same at length.

ACTION!="add|change", GOTO="linexinbar_input_end"

# The second-generation Steam Controller: `1302` on a cable, `1303` over
# Bluetooth, `1304` through its puck, `1305` through a Steam Machine's own
# receiver.
#
# Before Linux 7.3 `hid-steam` claims only the older controllers and the Deck,
# so this one falls through to `hid-generic` and never grows a joystick node;
# from 7.3, and earlier on handheld kernels, `hid-steam` drives it the way it
# drives the Deck, with a gamepad that stays silent in lizard mode and goes
# away when anything opens the raw node. So the shell reads its input report
# straight from hidraw, unless `hid-steam` drives it with `lizard_mode` off,
# where the kernel's own gamepad answers from the first press and is used
# instead. See "Which driver" in `crates/lxb-desktop/src/steam_hid.rs`.
#
# hidraw nodes are 0600 root:root by default and systemd's own uaccess rules
# tag none of them, so this is the whole of what makes that pad readable. The
# wireless dongle presents one interface per pad slot and every one of them
# reports the same pair, which is why this matches the device rather than a
# node number.
#
# Read-only is all the shell wants — writing feature reports, which is what
# turning lizard mode off means, stays Steam's business — but read-only is not
# on offer. systemd's `uaccess` builtin grants `rw` and takes no argument saying
# otherwise, so the ACL below carries the write bit whatever the mode says, and
# anything running as the user can send this pad feature reports. The only way
# to withhold it is a group with a mode, which is broader in the dimension that
# was worth narrowing. The mode below is the conventional one for a pad; the ACL
# is what actually grants the node.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="28de", ATTRS{idProduct}=="130[2-5]", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# The same pad over Bluetooth, which has no USB parent to carry those
# attributes. The pair is in the HID device's own kernel name, uppercase,
# between the bus number and the interface.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="*28DE:*130[2-5]*", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# The Steam Deck's controls, which the kernel does drive, and which the shell
# reads from hidraw all the same.
#
# `hid-steam` leaves the Deck's firmware typing — the buttons arrive as a
# keyboard, which is how the shell came to believe every Deck owner had one in
# their hands — and takes its own gamepad away the moment anything opens the
# Deck's raw node, which Steam does whenever it runs. The report on that node
# is the one thing that answers in both states, so the shell reads it, gives
# every other program the kernel's gamepad back as a stand-in, and the
# compositor drops the typing. See `steam_hid.rs`.
#
# Steam's own udev rules grant every Valve node too, where they are installed;
# this is for a Deck without them, where the node is root's alone and the
# Deck would have no controls at all once the compositor stopped listening to
# its keyboard. The controls are on an internal USB bus, so the device's own
# attributes match them.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="28de", ATTRS{idProduct}=="1205", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# And `/dev/uinput`, which is how the shell hands controllers back to the rest
# of the machine.
#
# Two things need it and they are the same trade from opposite ends. A pad the
# kernel does drive is taken away from every application, has its Guide button
# read off, and is republished as a device one button short — see `pad_guard`.
# A pad read from hidraw — the Steam Controller 2 and the Deck — is published as
# the gamepad applications would otherwise not have — see `steam_stand_in`. Without this node neither
# happens: in the first case the guard gives up and leaves the pad alone, which
# is a working controller with the guide button reaching games as well as the
# shell, and in the second there is no controller at all.
#
# `static_node` because the module is often not loaded when udev reads its
# rules, and a rule for a node that does not exist yet does nothing.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"

LABEL="linexinbar_input_end"
